## DBsubject(Mechanics)
## DBchapter(Oscillatory Motion and Waves)
## DBsection(Simple Harmonic Motion: A Special Periodic Motion)
## Date(December 2017)
## Institution(Brock University)
## Author(Brandon Lostracco)
## Edited (Connor Wilson, May 1, 2018)
## TitleText1('College Physics')
## AuthorText1('Urone et. al')
## EditionText1('2017')
## Section1('16.3')
## Problem1('5')
## KEYWORDS(force, equilibrium, Hooke's, harmonic, Newton, law, spring)

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context('Numeric');

$mass  = random(0.1, 0.3, 0.01);
$force = random(8,   12,  0.1);

$A1 = ($mass) * (9.80) / ($force);
$A2 = (($force) / ($mass))**(1 / 2) * ($A2);

BEGIN_PGML
An object is attached to a spring at its unloaded rest position and allowed to bounce up and down.

a)  If the spring has a force constant of [`[$force]`] [`\textrm{N/m}`] and a [`[$mass]`] [`\textrm{kg}`] mass object is set in motion as described, find the amplitude of the oscillations.

    [_]{$A1} [`\textrm{m}`]

b) Find the maximum velocity.

    [_]{$A2} [`\textrm{m/s}`]

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_HINT
a) Consider solving this problem using conservation of energy. What must be the the <i>total</i> stretch of the spring after all of the object's potential energy has been translated from gravitational to elastic? How does this total stretch relate to the amplitude of the object's oscillation?

b) Recall that the object attains its maximum velocity as it traverses the equilibrium position, the point at which the mechanical energy of the system is entirely kinetic.
END_PGML_HINT

ENDDOCUMENT();
